<article id="post-1548" class="post-1548 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-uncategorized">
	<header class="entry-header">
							<h1 class="entry-title"><a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/2016/08/we-forgot-a-pull-request-when-splitting-a-repo/" rel="bookmark">We forgot a Pull Request when Splitting a Repo</a></h1>			</header><!-- .entry-header -->

	
	
	<div class="entry-content">
			<p>We&#8217;re working on a little skunk works project at the office and it involved splitting a Github repository in two. After a successful split, I noticed we&#8217;d missed a PR that now impacts both repositories.</p>
<p>My head went to <code>git format-patch &lt;sha&gt;</code>, but I didn&#8217;t recall an option to target a file spec and at first glance it generates a patch for every commit after that SHA.</p>
<p><code>git show</code> on the other hand definitely advertises an option to filter<code>git show &lt;sha&gt; -- path/that/moved</code></p>
<p>Format that as <code>--pretty=email</code> and you can direct the output to a patch for</p>
<pre>git show --pretty=email &lt;sha&gt; -- path/that/moved&gt; pr-for-other-repo.patch
</pre>
<p>Now we can pull that into our other repo with <code> git am -3 pr-for-other-repo.patch</code>.</p>
<p>Turns out that <code>-- path/that/moved</code> semantic does work with <code>git format-patch</code>. So in the end, we could have just used</p>
<pre>git format-patch -1 -- path/that/moved
git am -3 partial-pr.patch
</pre>
				</div><!-- .entry-content -->

